azgor wrote:
> This is a collection of YANIs by users of the #nethack IRC channel on
> freenode.net.  Please excuse any old ideas that may be present.

Welcome back!

> [2]: Digging a pit and dropping an uncursed !oWater in it while
> levitating should make it into a swimming hole. --argiopeweb

That's nowhere near enough water.  However, having a limited ability to
make pools on demand would have many in game uses, plus would be
dangerous enough to be a good unknown item experimentation danger.
Maybe a potion of water in conjunction with some other item?  I'm sure
there's an item or two in 1st Edition AD&D that would fit the bill that
might be importable into Nethack.

> [3]: #dip a potion of water into an ice box to get a potion of ice;
> potions of ice in a container slow the decay of any corpses in that
> container --PsyMar

Hm.  Maybe not dipping, but it's an interesting idea.  However, I think
a good rule of thumb for adding new items is that they shouldn't be
added if they have *just* one game interaction.  What else would be
affected by ice?

> [4]: I like #3 so I'm going to extend on it. If you throw a potion of
> ice into lava, the lava solidifies --scwizard

That's not bad, and would be an additional case of the Wand of Cold.
(Which also brings up the idea that dipping a wand of cold into a
potion should freeze it....)

> [5]: role differentiation : give rangers some way (gained with level
> up? starting special magical instrument?) to charm animal monsters only

There are already magical instruments in the game that charm monsters.
Perhaps it should be a special case of that?

> [6]: Either you should never encounter your own ghost, or it should be
> peaceful if you encounter one. --azgor

It does seem a little weird to encounter your own ghost, but I take the
view that, whatever fantasy world that player imagines Nethack to take
place in, it is the one name that is guaranteed to be appropriate.

> [7]: Wood chucks should be able to pick up and throw wood golems.
> FalseLobster's idea. --nornagon

Kind of special-purpose, but I like it.  Of course, there are plenty of
other wooden items in the game they could find and throw.  But how much
wood would they throw, if they could do so?

> [8]: Rings of protection from shapechangers should be immune to being
> polymorphed --GreyKnight

Aaaah, clever!

> [9]: rusty weapon gives tetanus (from danderson) --iank

To who, the wielder?  I think this doesn't really add that much to the
game, weapon damage making them less effective is pretty bad as it is.

> [10]: Rings of teleport should teleport randomly about the level when
> on the ground, possibly teleporting their container (and the rest of
> its contents) along with it. When the =oTele is in inventory but is not
> worn, it would randomly change inventory letters. When a non-cursed
> =oTele is worn and while it is the only worn ring, it will randomly
> move to the other hand. --azgor

No, this would make it too easy to identify one of the major bad item
types.

> [11]: ...but if the ring is cursed, it will levelport. So don't put a
> cursed =oTele in your stash! --azgor

Same as above.

> [12]: Wand of Life - instantly kills the undead. Revives corpses. Fully
> heals other living targets. Can't be infinitely charged like /oD.
> --azgor

Some of this is already in the wand of undead turning.  Actually, all
wands cannot be infinitely charged, as their recharge count increases
their chance of exploding goes up.  A healing wand is potentially too
powerful, due to Nethack's support for zapping wands at oneself, but it
would be nice for keeping pets healthy.  Slash'em already has a wand of
healing.

> [13]: Some ability to carve statues would be nice.  A rock could be
> used for small monsters, and a boulder for larger ones.  Success would
> hinge on luck, wisdom, and dexterity.  These statues would never
> contain spellbooks, and could be brought to life by some form of stone
> to flesh. --argiopeweb

Kind of special case, but interesting.  Might be too powerful however,
unless the ability to carve statues was limited in some way....

> [14]: ...and a chisel should be added to carve with! --Gaulgath

....maybe by making chisels intrinsically limited-use items?

> [15]: an extension to 13/14, a pickaxe could be used to carve a statue,
> with a 50/50 chance (after the original chance of even being able to
> carve) of getting a "deformed statue" which would cause it to give you
> a corpse/dead monster message for the statue instead of a monster after
> stone to flesh. --argiopeweb

No, this shouldn't be possible, else people with a pickaxe (very common
in the Gnomish Mines) with a spellbook of stone to flesh, which are
very early in the game, could mint themselves experience points by
bringing to life statues of giant eels.

> [16]: A new class, skeptic, with deities L-Invisible Pink Unicorn,
> N-J.R. "Bob" Dobbs, C-Flying Spaghetti Monster. --dtriscuit

I move that all new-role YANIs must be developed in greater detail than
just suggesting deities.  What are the classes' powers?  How are they
different from the other guys?  What is their quest like?  What is
their quest artifact?

> [17]: (Adding to the skeptic idea) These deities wouldn't actually do
> anything and altars wouldn't work, making every game a mandatory
> atheist, but attempting to use any of these things should generate
> YAFM. --Tropsy

Well they have a drawback, but what advantages do they have?

> [18]: (even more to the skeptic idea) blessed could be replaced with
> 'well-made' and cursed with 'malfunctioning' or, even more extreme,
> everything skeptics touch become uncursed. --dtriscuit

Hmm... I'd definitely go with the more extreme idea here, it makes life
simultaneously easier and harder for them in an interesting way.

> [19]: Throwing armor or weapons at a pet that can use them should
> produce "Foo catches the bar." and have them make use of it. --Grunt

Means people wouldn't have to be careful with arrows around pets
though.

> [20]: Confused ?oEarth should fill the squares around the player, as
> well as the square he's standing on, with solid rock (in which the
> player becomes embedded) --lament

Way, way too dangerous!  No item should inflict automatic defeat on a
player, or people won't attempt to identify that item class through
experimentation!  This is a central tenent of roguelike design.  Note
that Nethack has no such items provided the player is in a suitably
survivable state before using the item and the player is canny enough.
Even amulets of strangulation can be survived by praying.

> [21]: Pet owners should be responsible for their pet's actions
> (angering shks, etc) if the pet is on a leash. --demontriscuit

Hmm, interesting... but are leashes overpowered as it is?  Do they need
to be make less useful?  There's already a number of checks on their
utility in the code, do we need more?

> [22]: Make people in pits near-immune to projectiles (~80% chance to
> miss), so that rangers and wizards can dig down to form bunkers. Hell
> yeah, son. --Azeral

This is a very good idea!  It should also work that way with any
distance attack that works in a straight line, like spells, wands or
breath attacks.

However, I'd say that the miss chance shouldn't apply if the monster is
adjacent to the pit (he's firing down, into it), that in such cases the
player should be hit TWICE by the effect if the shot is a ray-type (it
bounces around inside the pit!), and firing out of the pit will hit the
ceiling if the shot doesn't hit in an adjacent space (since if you're
in a pit, you have to fire upward out of it).  Wands of digging/spells
of dig could be a special case: if you're in a pit and zap, you create
a row of pits, potentially creating a channel for water or lava to flow
into.

Lots of potential here.

> [23]: If you #sit next to a tree, there should be a chance of being hit
> in the head by an apple; if this kills you, it should be "Killed by
> Newton's Fourth Law" --PsyMar

Potential food source, potential horse taming source.  But trees are
rare enough, and the application limited enough, that it's still
interesting.  But, are all Nethack trees apple trees?

> [24]: As a priest, give a demon price $2 when they ask for a bribe:
> "You give Asmodeus two bits for an ale." --Grunt

This would only apply if the demon prince were broke!  And we all know,
demons are not usually considered to be impoverished.

> [25]: A tin of whoop-ass. Does exactly what it says on the tin.
> --PsyMar

Although I have a certain fondness for this joke (and have an
Everything2 node to my credit in which I said this same thing), it's
not really good for the game.

Good set this time, overall.

- John H.



